1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,477
Module one: cardiac arrest and the Chain

2
00:00:06,477 --> 00:00:09,791
of Survival. This is the heart, beating

3
00:00:09,791 --> 00:00:11,559
about seventy times a minute.

4
00:00:12,412 --> 00:00:14,787
Each beat begins as an electrical impulse

5
00:00:14,787 --> 00:00:16,673
that spreads through the muscle, so the

6
00:00:16,673 --> 00:00:19,048
atria and then the ventricles contract in

7
00:00:19,048 --> 00:00:21,526
sequence, pushing blood into the aorta and

8
00:00:21,526 --> 00:00:22,828
the pulmonary trunk.

9
00:00:23,674 --> 00:00:25,924
Cardiac arrest is what happens when that

10
00:00:25,924 --> 00:00:27,988
electrical coordination fails.

11
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:32,907
In ventricular fibrillation, thousands of

12
00:00:32,907 --> 00:00:35,118
muscle fibres fire out of step.

13
00:00:35,958 --> 00:00:38,640
The ventricles stop contracting as one;

14
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:41,967
they quiver. A quivering ventricle ejects

15
00:00:41,967 --> 00:00:45,035
no blood, so cardiac output falls to zero,

16
00:00:45,035 --> 00:00:47,178
and within seconds the pulse is gone.

17
00:00:48,018 --> 00:00:50,132
The brain receives no oxygen:

18
00:00:50,132 --> 00:00:52,700
the patient becomes unresponsive and stops

19
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:54,654
breathing normally, sometimes with

20
00:00:54,654 --> 00:00:56,104
occasional gasps.

21
00:00:56,952 --> 00:00:58,645
This is a heart that has failed

22
00:00:58,645 --> 00:01:00,702
electrically, not a heart starved of

23
00:01:00,702 --> 00:01:02,952
blood, and that difference decides what

24
00:01:02,952 --> 00:01:03,902
you do next.

25
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:08,450
These two are often confused.

26
00:01:09,290 --> 00:01:11,824
In a heart attack, a coronary artery is

27
00:01:11,824 --> 00:01:13,779
blocked and part of the heart muscle is

28
00:01:13,779 --> 00:01:15,926
starved of blood, but the heart usually

29
00:01:15,926 --> 00:01:18,313
keeps beating: the patient is typically

30
00:01:18,313 --> 00:01:20,479
awake, often with chest pain.

31
00:01:21,296 --> 00:01:23,126
In cardiac arrest the problem is

32
00:01:23,126 --> 00:01:25,580
electrical, the heart stops pumping, and

33
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:28,348
the patient is unresponsive with no pulse.

34
00:01:29,198 --> 00:01:31,607
A heart attack can trigger cardiac arrest,

35
00:01:31,607 --> 00:01:34,057
but only arrest needs compressions now.

36
00:01:36,667 --> 00:01:39,031
Cardiac arrest has four possible rhythms,

37
00:01:39,031 --> 00:01:40,951
and the rhythm on the monitor decides the

38
00:01:40,951 --> 00:01:43,812
treatment. Two are shockable:

39
00:01:43,812 --> 00:01:46,107
ventricular fibrillation, and pulseless

40
00:01:46,107 --> 00:01:47,994
ventricular tachycardia, where the

41
00:01:47,994 --> 00:01:50,648
ventricles beat so fast they cannot fill.

42
00:01:51,487 --> 00:01:53,657
A shock depolarises the whole muscle at

43
00:01:53,657 --> 00:01:56,044
once, giving the heart's own pacemaker a

44
00:01:56,044 --> 00:01:58,226
chance to take over, and compressions

45
00:01:58,226 --> 00:01:59,630
continue around it.

46
00:02:00,469 --> 00:02:02,317
The other two are not shockable:

47
00:02:02,344 --> 00:02:04,617
pulseless electrical activity, with

48
00:02:04,617 --> 00:02:07,287
electrical activity but no pulse, and

49
00:02:07,287 --> 00:02:10,469
asystole, with no electrical activity at

50
00:02:10,469 --> 00:02:13,818
all. Here a shock cannot help, so

51
00:02:13,818 --> 00:02:15,773
compressions continue while the team

52
00:02:15,773 --> 00:02:17,927
searches for a reversible cause.

53
00:02:20,533 --> 00:02:22,426
How often do patients survive?

54
00:02:23,275 --> 00:02:26,695
Outside hospital, about 350,000 people a

55
00:02:26,695 --> 00:02:28,900
year in the United States have a cardiac

56
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:31,293
arrest, and nine in ten of them die.

57
00:02:32,137 --> 00:02:34,467
Overall survival to discharge is about

58
00:02:34,467 --> 00:02:40,002
10.5%. It is 21.1% in a public place,

59
00:02:40,002 --> 00:02:41,809
where someone is more likely to see the

60
00:02:41,809 --> 00:02:45,509
collapse and act, but only 8.9% at home.

61
00:02:46,357 --> 00:02:50,471
In hospital, survival is higher, 23.6% for

62
00:02:50,471 --> 00:02:53,925
adults and 45.2% for children, because the

63
00:02:53,925 --> 00:02:56,039
arrest is usually witnessed and help is

64
00:02:56,039 --> 00:03:00,048
close. Yet only about 41% of people

65
00:03:00,048 --> 00:03:02,094
receive compressions from a bystander

66
00:03:02,094 --> 00:03:03,476
before help arrives.

67
00:03:06,133 --> 00:03:08,178
That is why the nearest person matters

68
00:03:08,178 --> 00:03:11,715
most. Without circulation the brain begins

69
00:03:11,715 --> 00:03:13,976
to suffer within minutes, and every minute

70
00:03:13,976 --> 00:03:16,192
without compressions or a shock lowers the

71
00:03:16,192 --> 00:03:17,506
chance of survival.

72
00:03:18,337 --> 00:03:20,167
Chest compressions squeeze the heart

73
00:03:20,167 --> 00:03:22,326
between the sternum and the spine, pushing

74
00:03:22,326 --> 00:03:24,246
blood to the brain and to the heart muscle

75
00:03:24,246 --> 00:03:27,888
itself. Started immediately, compressions

76
00:03:27,888 --> 00:03:30,145
can double or even triple survival.

77
00:03:30,973 --> 00:03:33,200
In the first three minutes, the actions of

78
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:35,018
the nearest person matter more than

79
00:03:35,018 --> 00:03:36,616
everything that follows.

80
00:03:39,233 --> 00:03:42,631
The 2025 guidelines describe survival as

81
00:03:42,631 --> 00:03:44,824
one Chain of Survival, for adults and

82
00:03:44,824 --> 00:03:47,331
children, in hospital and outside it.

83
00:03:48,191 --> 00:03:50,039
Its six links run in order.

84
00:03:50,885 --> 00:03:52,942
Early recognition and activation:

85
00:03:52,942 --> 00:03:55,158
someone recognises the arrest and calls

86
00:03:55,158 --> 00:03:58,362
for help. High-quality cardiopulmonary

87
00:03:58,362 --> 00:04:00,998
resuscitation: compressions, and breaths,

88
00:04:00,998 --> 00:04:02,578
especially for children and in

89
00:04:02,578 --> 00:04:04,107
opioid-related arrest.

90
00:04:04,937 --> 00:04:07,682
Defibrillation, as early as possible.

91
00:04:08,519 --> 00:04:11,026
Advanced resuscitation by the code team.

92
00:04:11,861 --> 00:04:13,686
Post-cardiac arrest care.

93
00:04:14,531 --> 00:04:16,617
And recovery and survivorship.

94
00:04:17,465 --> 00:04:19,938
Each link depends on the one before it.

95
00:04:22,567 --> 00:04:25,601
If you trained on the 2020 guidelines, you

96
00:04:25,601 --> 00:04:27,585
are updating, not relearning.

97
00:04:28,429 --> 00:04:30,561
Four separate chains became one.

98
00:04:31,411 --> 00:04:32,627
The terms changed:

99
00:04:32,627 --> 00:04:35,127
rescue breaths are now simply breaths, and

100
00:04:35,127 --> 00:04:37,600
ventilation now means mechanical support.

101
00:04:38,449 --> 00:04:41,665
The compression rate stays at 100 to 120 a

102
00:04:41,665 --> 00:04:43,585
minute, and single-rescuer adult

103
00:04:43,585 --> 00:04:45,949
resuscitation stays at 30 compressions to

104
00:04:45,949 --> 00:04:46,797
2 breaths.

105
00:04:49,400 --> 00:04:52,638
To summarise. Cardiac arrest is an

106
00:04:52,638 --> 00:04:54,854
electrical failure that stops the heart's

107
00:04:54,854 --> 00:04:57,953
output. Two of its four rhythms can be

108
00:04:57,953 --> 00:05:01,291
shocked. And survival depends on six

109
00:05:01,291 --> 00:05:03,286
links, the first of which is you.

110
00:05:04,136 --> 00:05:06,204
In the next module, you will learn to

111
00:05:06,204 --> 00:05:08,670
recognise deterioration and arrest, and

112
00:05:08,670 --> 00:05:10,154
how to call for help.

113
00:05:12,767 --> 00:05:14,535
Keep learning with MedSkills.

